# Shell scripts must reach the working tree with LF line endings.
#
# This is the INVERSE of the rule that used to live here. That one forced CRLF
# on *.bat, because cmd.exe mis-parses an LF-terminated batch file containing a
# non-ASCII byte under codepage 65001. The batch files are gone; shell scripts
# replaced them, and their failure mode is worse.
#
# A CRLF shell script does not mis-parse -- it does not run at all. The kernel
# reads the shebang literally, so `#!/usr/bin/env bash\r` asks for an
# interpreter named "bash\r" and the script dies with
#     bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env bash^M: no such file or directory
# which names neither the real problem nor the file that caused it.
#
# core.autocrlf=true is a per-USER setting and is true on the Windows dev box,
# so without this rule a fresh clone there produces broken scripts. Pinning it
# in the repo is what makes it independent of whose machine did the checkout.
#
# Guarded by tools/tests/test_shell_line_endings.py.
*.sh text eol=lf

# systemd units, if ever committed, are read by a C daemon on Linux only.
*.service text eol=lf
*.target  text eol=lf
